// An out-binding `(k:)` on a call is a NEW binding (colon = declare) — it legitimately SHADOWS a name the
// enclosing map already bound in its write-back list `(k)`. The call's result lands on the throwaway shadow,
// so the queried column is never written and keeps its seeded value (`k=0`). This is sound, but the silent
// lost write is a trap, so the compiler warns that the write-back column `k` is a DEAD binding
// (`dead_write_binding`, W0031): it is declared writable but never actually written. To write the column,
// drop the colon (`mark()(k)`); to bind a fresh local, rename it.
//
// `mark` is a value-producing effect: `sys_write` returns its byte count (3), which the `(k:)` shadow
// captures and discards. The RUN lines assert the warning fires at build and the program prints `k=0`.
// RUN: %arche build -o %t %s 2>&1 | grep -qF "dead_write_binding"
// RUN: %t 2>/dev/null | grep -qx "k=0"
#import { fmt os }

mark :: func() -> Eff(i64) {
  return os.sys_write(2, "..\n", 3);
}

k :: i64;
B :: arche { k }
[1]B(1);

seed :: system {
  B.k = { 0 };
}

// `k:` re-declares a NEW `k` shadowing the map's queried + write-bound `k`; mark's 3 lands on the shadow, so
// the queried column is never written and keeps 0 — and `dead_write_binding` warns about the unwritten `(k)`.
bad :: system eff {
  map (query { k })(k) eff {
    mark()(k:);
  };
}

show :: system eff {
  map (query { k }) eff {
    fmt.printf("k=%d\n", k);
  };
}

#run seq({ seed, bad, show })
